    content: "Consider the Star Trek transporter or a sci-fi atomic teleporter.\n\nYou step into a booth on Earth. A scanner reads the quantum state and spatial position of every atom in your body, including your memory weights and neural patterns. The machine converts this data into a digital file, vaporizes your original physical body on Earth, beams the signal to Mars at the speed of light, and reconstructs an exact physical copy using local Martian raw materials.\n\nThe person stepping out of the Martian booth has your memories, your childhood scars, your tone of voice, your preference for black coffee, and your intention to finish reading this essay. To everyone else (your friends, spouse, bank, and government) you have arrived safely on Mars.\n\nFrom a third-person perspective, psychological continuity is 100% intact.\n\nFrom a first-person perspective, did you travel to Mars, or were you murdered on Earth while a high-resolution duplicate was born with your memories?\n\n> [!definition] Spatiotemporal Continuity\n> The philosophical requirement that personal identity over time depends on an unbroken physical trajectory through space and time, rather than mere digital copying of information.\n\n## The Faulty Teleporter\n\nDerek Parfit highlighted the absurdity of this problem by altering the thought experiment: What if the machine on Earth has a mechanical fault? It successfully scans your body and creates the Martian copy, but fails to vaporize the original on Earth.\n\nNow there are two physical bodies. Earth-You is sitting in the booth, perfectly healthy, being told by the technician that the disintegrator jammed while your copy stepped out on Mars. Mars-You is stepping onto the Martian landing pad, feeling happy the trip went smoothly.\n\nAt this moment, where is your subjective consciousness?\n\nYour first-person experience cannot inhabit both bodies at once. You will not experience a split-screen view of Earth and Mars. Earth-You remains locked inside the skull on Earth, looking at the broken machine. Mars-You is locked inside the skull on Mars.\n\n> [!intuition] The First-Person Test\n> If Earth-You lives for another fifty years and dies of old age, Mars-You was never \"you\" in terms of subjective continuity: it was a twin convinced it was you. And if the technician shoots Earth-You in the head after the scan, the duplicate on Mars doesn't make Earth-You any less dead.\n\n## Informational vs. Physical Continuity\n\nWe naturally default to the Psychological Continuity View: we assume that as long as memory, personality, and brain structure survive, we survive. But our deepest subjective intuition relies on Spatiotemporal Continuity: an unbroken physical thread moving through time and space.\n\nDigital brain uploading, mind-cloning, and atom-streaming all sell the promise of immortality. But until we understand whether first-person experience requires physical continuity or merely informational equivalence, uploading your mind might be nothing more than paying a tech company to replace you with an actor who sounds just like you."
