    dek: "Science demands public third-person data, yet subjective experience is strictly first-person, creating an epistemological crisis for mind science.",
    content: "The gold standard of scientific evidence is third-person replicability: an observation must be accessible to any qualified observer with the right instruments, regardless of who or where they are.\n\nIf I claim that water boils at 100°C at sea level, you don't have to take my word for it. You can set up a thermometer, boil water, and read the instrument yourself. The data is public.\n\nConsciousness breaks this methodology entirely.\n\nMy experience of red, my feeling of grief, my sensation of physical pain, and the qualitative texture of a dream are exclusively first-person phenomena. You cannot observe my pain; you can only observe my facial expressions, listen to my voice, or scan my brain on an fMRI machine.\n\nThose brain scans and physical movements are public data. But the pain itself, the actual subjective feeling, remains strictly private.\n\n> [!definition] First-Person Asymmetry\n> The epistemological wall where third-person science measures physical correlates, while the actual qualitative experience remains exclusively accessible to the first-person subject.\n\n## The Historic Conflict\n\nThis creates a deep epistemological crisis for the scientific study of mind: Can private, first-person experience ever qualify as rigorous evidence?\n\nEarly 20th-century psychology tried to solve this by splitting into two extreme camps.\n\nIntrospectionism tried to train subjects to carefully observe and report their inner sensations. It collapsed because different laboratories produced inconsistent inner reports that could not be objectively verified.\n\nBehaviorism declared subjective experience completely unscientific and banished it from psychology altogether. Behaviorists argued that science should only measure observable inputs and outputs, treating the mind as a black box. Behaviorism was absurd because it denied the very thing we are most certain of, and it eventually fell apart during the cognitive revolution.\n\n> [!insight] Modern Approaches\n> **Heterophenomenology**: Treat first-person verbal reports as texts to be interpreted rather than direct observations.  \n> **Neurophenomenology**: Train subjects in disciplined introspection and correlate reports with real-time neural imaging.\n\n## The Epistemological Wall\n\nConsider how medicine handles pain. A doctor cannot measure pain with a blood draw or an X-ray. They hand the patient a scale from 1 to 10. That number is a first-person introspective report. It is subjective, noisy, and prone to individual variance. Yet modern neurology and pharmacology could not function for a single day without treating that private report as real clinical evidence.\n\nWe live with a permanent epistemological asymmetry: third-person data gives us public access to relations and measurements, while excluding the inner feel of existence. First-person data gives us immediate access to experience, but cannot be directly shared or calibrated.\n\nIf science insists that only public, third-person data can count as evidence, it condemns itself to studying everything in the universe except the very light by which we know the universe exists."
