    depth: "Longform",
    dek: "Under Bayesian permissivism, two epistemic peers can examine identical data and rationally land on opposite conclusions.",
    content: "We tend to assume that when two intelligent people look at the exact same evidence and reach opposite conclusions, at least one of them must be broken.\n\nOne person must be falling for a cognitive bias, acting in bad faith, misinterpreting the data, or holding back hidden motives. We tell ourselves: \"If they just saw the data as clearly as I do, they would agree with me.\"\n\nThis assumption is comfortable, flattering, and deeply mistaken.\n\nIn epistemology, this problem is known as Peer Disagreement: What happens when two epistemic peers (people who are equally intelligent, equally well-informed, and equally careful) examine an identical body of evidence and land on opposite conclusions?\n\n> [!definition] Epistemic Permissivism\n> The view that a body of evidence is often complex and ambiguous enough to permit multiple distinct, rationally justified belief states among epistemic peers.\n\n## The Two Philosophical Camps\n\nThere are two major philosophical camps on whether this is even logically possible.\n\nThe Uniqueness Thesis asserts that for any given body of evidence, there is exactly one rational attitude to take toward a proposition. Evidence is a strict constraint. If two people disagree on identical evidence, at least one of them has committed an error.\n\nPermissivism asserts that a body of evidence is often rich and ambiguous enough to permit multiple distinct rational belief states. Two people can look at identical data and rationally land on opposite sides.\n\nHow can Permissivism be true without devolving into relativism?\n\nThe answer lies in Bayesian Inference. According to Bayes' Theorem, your posterior belief depends not only on new evidence, but also on your prior probabilities: the background framework of assumptions and plausibility weights you bring to the table before encountering the evidence.\n\nTwo expert economists can examine identical inflation and interest-rate data. Economist A carries a prior framework prioritizing monetary velocity; Economist B carries a prior framework prioritizing labor market dynamics. When new evidence arrives, both update their beliefs flawlessly using Bayes' rule. Yet Economist A concludes inflation is transitory, while Economist B concludes it is structural. Neither economist made a mistake; their divergence was built into their initial prior frameworks.\n\n> [!warning] Conciliationism vs. Steadfastness\n> **Conciliationism**: Discovering peer disagreement is higher-order evidence that should reduce your confidence and force you to split the difference.  \n> **Steadfastness**: Your first-order rational evaluation of primary evidence matters more than social symmetry; don't downgrade a well-justified belief.\n\n## Reasoning Under Uncertainty\n\nI find myself leaning toward Permissivism, but with a sobering implication.\n\nIf Permissivism is true, then on the most interesting human questions in ethics, economics, technology forecasting, and politics, reason alone will never force a universal consensus. The evidence is simply too complex, and human priors are too diverse.\n\nDeep disagreement is not a sign of human malice or intellectual failure. It is a fundamental feature of reasoning under uncertainty."
