    content: "The Roman poet Lucretius posed one of the most elegant philosophical paradoxes about mortality over two thousand years ago in *De Rerum Natura*:\n\n> \"Look back again at how the endless ages of time past before our birth have been nothing to us. Nature holds this up as a mirror of the time to come after our eventual death. Does anything appear horrible in that? Does anything seem gloomy?\"\n\n> [!definition] The Symmetry Argument\n> Lucretius's observation that post-mortem nonexistence after death is logically identical to ante-natal nonexistence before birth, rendering fear of death irrational.\n\n## The Two Abysses\n\nConsider two vast abysses of nonexistence: the 13.8 billion years of cosmic history that elapsed before you were born, and the infinite billions of years that will elapse after you die.\n\nSymmetrically, both states are identical. In both cases, you experience no pain, no darkness, no boredom, and no regret. You simply do not exist.\n\nYet almost no one lies awake at 3:00 AM filled with existential dread about the fact that they did not exist during the reign of Julius Caesar or the Jurassic period. We look back at our pre-natal nonexistence with complete emotional indifference.\n\nYet we look forward to our post-mortem nonexistence with terror, grief, or anxiety.\n\nWhy the asymmetry? Is our emotional reaction rational, or is it a psychological glitch?\n\n> [!intuition] Deprivation vs. Future Bias\n> Thomas Nagel argued that death deprives us of future life, whereas pre-natal nonexistence deprives us of nothing. But Derek Parfit showed that humans carry an innate evolutionary temporal bias toward the future: we dread future pain while remaining indifferent to past pain.\n\nEvolutionary biology explains why this bias exists: natural selection cares deeply about future threats because future actions affect survival and reproduction. Past nonexistence poses zero risk to genes; future nonexistence is the end of the line.\n\nLucretius was logically right: post-mortem nonexistence is no more painful than pre-natal nonexistence. But logic is no match for evolutionary engineering. We are wired to fear the void ahead because our ancestors survived by dreading the end of time."
