    content: "Consider two scenarios:\n\nIn Scenario A, Smith stands to inherit a large fortune if his six-year-old cousin dies. One evening while the child is taking a bath, Smith sneaks into the bathroom, drowns the child, and arranges the scene to look like an accident.\n\nIn Scenario B, Jones stands to inherit a large fortune if his six-year-old cousin dies. One evening while the child is taking a bath, Jones sneaks into the bathroom planning to drown him. But just as Jones enters, the child slips, strikes his head, and falls face down in the water. Jones stands quietly by the tub, watching the child drown, doing nothing to save him when he easily could have.\n\nPhilosopher James Rachels introduced this thought experiment to challenge the Doctrine of Doing and Allowing: the widely held intuition that actively causing harm is far more morally monstrous than failing to prevent harm.\n\n> [!definition] Omission Bias\n> The evolutionary cognitive tendency to judge harmful inactions (omissions) as morally less severe than harmful actions (commissions), even when motives and outcomes are identical.\n\n## The Consequentialist View\n\nFrom a pure Consequentialist or Utilitarian perspective, the moral weight of an action is determined entirely by its consequences. If Person X dies because I pushed them off a cliff, or if Person X dies because I watched them drown without throwing a life preserver, the net state of the world is identical: a human life was lost. To a utilitarian, failing to save is morally equivalent to killing.\n\nPeter Singer famously applied this logic to global poverty in *Famine, Affluence, and Morality*. Singer argued that if a wealthy adult walks past a shallow pond and sees a child drowning, they are morally obligated to wade in and save the child, even if it ruins their expensive shoes. By extension, if a wealthy adult chooses to buy luxury items instead of donating to a high-impact charity that could save a child from preventable disease, they are letting that child die just as surely as the person standing by the shallow pond.\n\n> [!insight] Why Omission Bias Evolved\n> In small hunter-gatherer bands, active violence was easy to detect and punish. Inaction was ambient and hard to track. Our moral psychology evolved to treat omissions as less severe so we wouldn't be emotionally paralyzed by every tragedy we couldn't prevent.\n\n## The Problem of Inviolable Rights\n\nDeontological ethical systems defend the distinction for another reason: individual agency. If failing to prevent harm is morally identical to causing harm, then we are all perpetually committing moral atrocities every second of the day. Every dollar spent on coffee, every hour spent sleeping, could theoretically be spent preventing a death somewhere on Earth.\n\nIf utilitarian omission logic is pushed to its extreme, individual freedom, privacy, and personal projects vanish. We become mere utility-calculating machinery.\n\nWe are left with an agonizing ethical rift: logic and consequentialism insist that sitting by while preventable harm occurs makes us accomplices to the outcome, while human psychology and deontological ethics insist that we must distinguish between the harms we actively cause and the tragic harms we merely witness."
