    content: "We like to think of individual human reason as a solitary superpower: a miniature scientific laboratory inside the mind designed to evaluate facts, weigh evidence, and discover truth.\n\nWhen psychologists demonstrate that humans are riddled with confirmation bias, myside bias, and emotional rationalization, we treat these quirks as bugs or flaws in human cognition. We ask: Why is the brain so bad at being logical?\n\nCognitive scientists Hugo Mercier and Dan Sperber offered a radical answer in *The Enigma of Reason*: We are asking the wrong question because we misunderstand what reason evolved to do.\n\n> [!definition] Argumentative Theory of Reasoning\n> Hugo Mercier and Dan Sperber's thesis that human reason evolved not for solitary truth-seeking, but as a social tool to persuade others, evaluate claims made by rivals, and justify status.\n\n## Evolutionary Features of Social Reason\n\nReason did not evolve to help isolated individuals discover abstract truth in solitude. Reason evolved as a specialized social instrument to persuade others, evaluate arguments offered by rivals, and justify our social standing within a group.\n\nWhen you look at human cognition through an evolutionary and social lens, the supposed flaws of human reasoning suddenly look like highly optimized features:\n\nFirst, **Confirmation Bias** acts as an efficiency mechanism. If reason’s function is to help you win arguments in a social group, searching for counter-evidence against your own stance is a waste of metabolic energy. Confirmation bias allows you to gather arguments for your side while outsourcing the job of finding counter-evidence to your opponents.\n\nSecond, there is a stark **Asymmetry Between Production and Evaluation**. Humans are notoriously terrible at evaluating their own arguments, but exceptionally good at spotting flaws in other people's arguments. This asymmetry makes perfect sense if reason is a social tool: you produce biased arguments to push your agenda, while your peers exert strict quality control by shooting down your weak claims.\n\nThird, we engage in **Identity-Protective Cognition**. Dan Kahan’s research shows that when scientific facts conflict with group values, more scientifically literate people use their intelligence to construct more sophisticated rationalizations to defend their group's identity.\n\n> [!insight] Why Tribal Belonging Trumps Fact-Checking\n> In human evolutionary history, being ostracized from your tribe for holding an unpopular truth meant physical death. Being wrong about an abstract scientific fact has zero impact on daily survival, but being cast out of your social group destroys your life. Preserving social belonging is far more evolutionary rational than holding an unpopular truth.\n\n## Designing Institutional Social Environments\n\nIndividual human minds left to themselves will almost never default to dispassionate truth-seeking. We are wired to use intellect as a sword for social competition and a shield for tribal belonging.\n\nHow did humanity ever manage to build science, philosophy, and legal systems under these conditions?\n\nWe did it by engineering institutional social environments that hijack our social psychology. Peer review forces scientists to face hostile evaluation by rivals. Adversarial legal systems assign opposing lawyers to present the strongest biased arguments to a neutral jury.\n\nTruth is rarely discovered inside a single human head. Truth is a collective byproduct that emerges when we force biased, socially-driven minds to compete in environments with strict rules of engagement."
