    content: "The economic rationale for patent protection in the pharmaceutical industry is straightforward:\n\nDeveloping a novel life-saving drug costs roughly $1 billion to $2.5 billion once you factor in clinical trial failures, regulatory compliance, and basic research. Once the molecular structure of that drug is published, generic manufacturers can synthesize the chemical compound for pennies per pill.\n\nWithout temporary patent monopolies granting high pricing power, private capital would never invest billions of dollars to discover new medicine. The drug would be copied instantly, price would collapse to marginal manufacturing cost, and the innovating company would go bankrupt.\n\nPatents solve the upfront R&D incentive problem. But in doing so, they create a severe structural paradox: The economic incentives created by patent monopolies actively distort the goals of medicine.\n\n> [!definition] Health Innovation Prize Funds\n> Joseph Stiglitz's proposed alternative to drug patents, where governments grant cash prizes for cures while making the underlying formulas open-source generic medicine.\n\n## Three Structural Economic Failures\n\nConsider three distinct economic failures driven by the current pharmaceutical patent model:\n\nFirst, companies engage in **Evergreening and Patent Thickets**. Instead of competing to discover genuinely novel therapies, pharmaceutical firms make trivial chemical modifications to an expiring patent (such as altering a salt formulation or combining two existing molecules into one pill) and secure an additional 20 years of monopoly protection. In 2018, the maker of Humira filed over 240 patent applications in the US to create a patent thicket that prevented low-cost biosimilar competition for decades.\n\nSecond, there is a stark **Asymmetry Between Chronic Care and Cures**. From a purely financial perspective, a drug that cures a disease with a single dose is a terrible business model compared to a drug that manages a chronic disease for thirty years. A cure eliminates your customer base; a daily maintenance pill creates a recurring subscription revenue stream. In 2018, a Goldman Sachs biotech analyst report asked the unvarnished question: \"Is curing patients a sustainable business model?\" citing Gilead's cure for Hepatitis C, which caused revenues to fall as patients were cured.\n\nThird, we face an **Access Crisis in Low-Income Markets**. Under patent monopoly pricing, life-saving drugs are priced to maximize profit in high-income markets like the United States. This automatically prices out billions of people in low- and middle-income nations who cannot afford monopoly rents, even though the marginal cost of manufacturing an extra dose is virtually zero.\n\n> [!insight] Alternative Incentive Frameworks\n> **Prize Funds**: Reward developers with lump-sum cash prizes for breakthroughs, while placing formulas immediately in the public domain for generic manufacturing.  \n> **Advance Market Commitments**: Guarantee government purchases for critical vaccines in developing nations to de-risk upfront R&D.\n\nThe drug patent is an artificial market construct designed centuries ago. We do not have to accept a system where financial returns depend on prolonging sickness. We can align private innovation with human health, if we have the courage to redesign the rules of the game."
