    dek: "High-trust societies are not built on naive goodwill, but on Axelrod's Tit-for-Tat, Schelling's credible commitments, and Spence's costly signaling.",
    content: "We are taught to view trust as a soft, moral emotion: a feeling of warm goodwill that exists between people of good character. We praise honesty, preach empathy, and lament the decline of social trust in modern society.\n\nGame theory takes a much colder, more useful view: Naive goodwill is an unstable equilibrium.\n\nIf a society relies entirely on moral exhortation and good intentions to maintain cooperation, it remains perpetually vulnerable to exploitation. A single defecting actor can extract immense wealth from a naive, trusting population, causing the entire cooperative ecosystem to collapse.\n\nHigh-trust societies are not built by telling people to be nicer. High-trust societies are built through enforceable game-theoretic architecture: systems where cooperation becomes the individual's most rational strategic move.\n\n> [!definition] Credible Commitment\n> Thomas Schelling's game-theoretic insight that intentionally restricting your future choices (such as burning your ships) paradoxically strengthens your bargaining power and enforces trust.\n\n## Three Game-Theoretic Pillars of Trust\n\nTo understand how enforceable trust works, we must look at three core concepts from game theory:\n\nFirst, **The Iterated Prisoner's Dilemma & Tit-for-Tat**. In a one-off Prisoner's Dilemma, the dominant strategy is to defect. Everything changes when the game is repeated with an unknown end date. Robert Axelrod's computer tournaments showed that the winning strategy was Tit-for-Tat: start by cooperating, defect immediately if the opponent defects, and forgive as soon as they resume cooperation. Tit-for-Tat succeeded because it punished defection instantly while making cooperation profitable.\n\nSecond, **Credible Commitments**. Nobel laureate Thomas Schelling showed that limiting your options paradoxically increases your power. The classic historical example is Hernán Cortés burning his ships upon landing in Mexico, making retreat physically impossible. In modern markets, legal contracts, escrows, performance bonds, and collateral are the equivalent of burning your ships.\n\nThird, **Costly Signaling**. Michael Spence’s Signaling Theory demonstrates that for a signal to establish genuine trust between strangers, the signal must be costly enough that an imposter cannot afford to fake it. When a company offers a 10-year money-back warranty, it sends a costly signal that its product does not break. An imposter selling junk would go bankrupt offering the same warranty.\n\n> [!takeaway] The Architecture of Civilization\n> Institutions like legal courts, property registries, escrows, and credit ratings are not altruistic projects. They are mechanisms for converting one-off Prisoner's Dilemmas into iterated games with costly signals and credible commitments.\n\nMorality asks us to be good. Game theory builds the stage where being good is the only sensible way to play."
